    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite passes: **58 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented soft-delete for owners so `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` now flags the owner rather than removing the row, and the create endpoint's duplicate/identity checks ignore flagged owners.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `deleted` (`Boolean`) field mapped to a new `deleted` column, with getter/setter.\n\n2. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct auto-maps it in `toOwnerDto`. GET responses now carry `deleted`.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` column to the `owners` table in the hsqldb (test DB), h2, postgres, and mysql schema files. Updated the positional `INSERT INTO owners VALUES (...)` rows in `db/hsqldb/data.sql` to include the new `FALSE` value (h2/postgres use explicit column lists, so their inserts were unaffected).\n\n4. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**:\n   - `deleteOwner` now sets `deleted = true` and saves the owner (returns `204`, a 2xx), instead of deleting the record.\n   - `addOwner` sets `deleted = false` on newly created owners.\n   - Added an `isNotDeleted` helper and applied it to filter out deleted owners in the three duplicate/identity checks (`requireUniqueIdentity`, `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate`, and `findPossibleDuplicate`), so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only matching owner was soft-deleted.\n\nThe new checkpoint tests (Cp46) and all 58 existing tests pass.\n\nNote: the soft-deleted owners now remain visible in `GET /api/owners` (list) and are still counted by the city-capacity / daily-limit / namesake / household-count logic in `ClinicServiceImpl`, since the spec scoped the exclusion specifically to the create endpoint's duplicate and identity checks. If you'd like deleted owners hidden from listing or excluded from those other counts too, that's a straightforward follow-up.",
